%I A400487 #9 Oct 07 2026 17:19:46
%S A400487 0,0,0,4,13,32,69,147,303,620,1270,2618,5340,10802,21925,44511,90024,
%T A400487 182048,367482,740758,1491673,3003711,6045633,12159386,24450352,
%U A400487 49147890,98764708,198420345,398515611
%N A400487 Number of positive integers k < 2^n that take the same number of Collatz steps to reach 1 as k-1 or k+1.
%C A400487 The steps are those of the Collatz map x -> x/2 for even x, 3*x+1 for odd x (A006370), and their number is A006577; 0 never reaches 1. Equivalently, a(n) counts the k < 2^n such that k or k-1 is in A078417, that is, the k < 2^n lying in a run of at least two consecutive integers that take the same number of steps.
%C A400487 Wu (1992) computed a(n) for n <= 24, observed that a(n)/(2^n-1) increases with n, and conjectured that the proportion of integers lying in such runs tends to 1.
%C A400487 If a(n)/2^n -> 1, then the positive integers that reach 1 have natural density 1, because every k counted reaches 1. Whether they do is an open problem; the best known lower bound for the number of k <= x that reach 1 is x^0.84 (Krasikov and Lagarias).
%C A400487 The ratio a(n)/(2^n-1) is 0.606 for n = 10, 0.725 for n = 24 and 0.732 for n = 26, and increases on this range.
%H A400487 Ilia Krasikov and Jeffrey C. Lagarias, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0205002">Bounds for the 3x+1 problem using difference inequalities</a>, arXiv:math/0205002 [math.NT], 2002.
%H A400487 Jeffrey C. Lagarias, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0309224">The 3x+1 problem: An annotated bibliography (1963-1999) (sorted by author)</a>, arXiv:math/0309224 [math.NT], 2003-2011 (entry 188).
%H A400487 Jia-Bang Wu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13245/j.hust.1992.05.030">A tendency of the proportion of consecutive numbers of the same height in the Collatz problem</a> (in Chinese), J. Huazhong Univ. Sci. Tech. 20 (1992), no. 5, 171-174.
%F A400487 a(n) = #{1 <= k < 2^n: k is in A078417 or k-1 is in A078417}.
%e A400487 a(4) = 4: below 16 the numbers that take the same number of steps as a neighbor are 12 and 13 (9 steps each) and 14 and 15 (17 steps each).
%o A400487 (Python)
%o A400487 def h(k):
%o A400487     s = 0
%o A400487     while k != 1:
%o A400487         k = k//2 if k % 2 == 0 else 3*k+1
%o A400487         s += 1
%o A400487     return s
%o A400487 def a(n):
%o A400487     H = [None] + [h(k) for k in range(1, 2**n + 1)]
%o A400487     return sum(1 for k in range(1, 2**n) if (k > 1 and H[k] == H[k-1]) or H[k] == H[k+1])
%o A400487 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 17)])
%Y A400487 Cf. A006370, A006577, A078417, A078441, A277109, A400486.
%K A400487 nonn,more,new
